A||||7||pontiacs4me||clutch questions||||1||
Z||000000||pontiacs4me||10-25-2001||04:21 PM||||here goes, last night i told about my friend that just bought a 87 GT.  It is a 5 spd, and he really wanted an auto, but i convinced him the car was a good deal, and he would pick up on how to drive a manual real fast.  well today that lovely clutch decided to retire.  what is the easiest way to go about pulling everything apart to change the clutch without dropping the cradle.  or do i have to do that?  any advice would be greatly appreciated.  thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||10-25-2001||06:31 PM||||Check out Ed Parks' website (the Fiero Factory).  Somewhere in there, he describes how to remove the transaxle w/o dropping the cradle.  It works great... I can tell you that, because I <B>just</B> pulled the Xaxle out of my 1986 SE V6 a couple days ago  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||Avery Montembeault||10-26-2001||12:38 AM||||whats the url for that site, I dont see it listed under the vendors (or maybe im just blind)||||reg||1
Z||000003||DR650SE||10-26-2001||01:52 AM|||| <A HREF="http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/clutch.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/clutch.htm</A> <BR>that is a link so you dont have to pull everything i think.  Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000004||pontiacs4me||10-26-2001||03:38 AM||||wow, what a pain in the @$$.  that was rediculous.  I havent had any experience with manuals before, so I get to learn on a fiero.  well in 12 hours we got everything pulled apart, the clutch in and the transmission bolted back to the engine.  Tomorrow we should be able to get the suspension and mounts put back together within 2 hours.  oh well, its almost done.  the most time consuming part was getting the shaft to line up in the clutch assembly.  heres a tip, take a long bolt, that is the same thread as the tranny to engine bolts, and screw it in one of the bottom holes, put the driver side axle into the tranny and have a buddy slide under the car and bench it up as close as he can get it to line up.  then taking your foot push on the tranny casing while turning the axle shaft.  It slid right in, and that was after 4 hours of fighting it from underneath and trying to get bolts to start.  rediculous.  I love autos!||||reg||1
Z||000005||pontiacs4me||10-26-2001||03:40 AM||||one more thing, when we took the old clutch out, it had absolutely no material on the metal backing.  there was a huge hairball in there that was the friction material, but only a metal plate was to be found inside.  rediculous!||||reg||1
Z||000006||stsmithgt||10-26-2001||09:59 AM||||Since it happened to me recently, be aware your car is old and weird things like to break.  Take a close look at he clutch fork, and associated parts, to determine they can make it another term between clutch replacement.  Probably not a problem, but a good time to look at it. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art.(in progress.)    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/carpicsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||10-26-2001||12:56 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B>...when we took the old clutch out, it had absolutely no material on the metal backing...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>I guess that means you got the most out of that clutch!  When I took mine out, 2 of the 4 cushion springs fell out of their slots in the disc, and they were shiny (scraped up a bit).  The disc was pretty much toast - at least I knew I hadn't torn into it for no reason!<P>FWIW, I used guide pins, too - except I beheaded & slotted a pair of bolts, and threaded them into the top 2 holes in the engine.  Figured that would keep the trans. from falling and bunging up the input splines, etc.  Should help when reinstalling, too.<P>BTW, pontiacs4me, thanks for sharing the tips on reinstalling the tranny - I am sure they will help me out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">       <P>||||reg||1
